zajjaj:63-63.t:
﴿قالَ بَلْ فَعَلَهُ كَبِيرُهم هَذا﴾ — meaning the great idol. ﴿فاسْألُوهم إنْ كانُوا يَنْطِقُونَ﴾ — some said the meaning is only: ”بَلْ فَعَلَهُ كَبِيرُهم هَذا إنْ كانُوا يَنْطِقُونَ“ ("rather, this biggest one of them did it — if they can speak"). It has come in the exegesis that Ibrāhīm uttered three statements other than what their wording requires, because of the benefit in that. They are his saying: ﴿فَقالَ إنِّي سَقِيمٌ﴾ [al-Ṣāffāt: 89];
his saying: ﴿فَعَلَهُ كَبِيرُهم هَذا﴾;
and his saying: ”إنَّ سارَةَ أُخْتِي“ ("Sāra is my sister"). All three have a clear aspect of truthfulness: Sāra was his sister in religion.
His saying ﴿إنِّي سَقِيمٌ﴾ [al-Ṣāffāt: 89] has more than one interpretation. One of them: ”إنِّي مُغْتَمٌّ بِضَلالَتِكُمْ، حَتّى أنا كالسَّقِيمِ“ ("I am so distressed by your misguidance that I am like one who is sick"). Another: ”إنِّي سَقِيمٌ عِنْدَكُمْ“ ("I am sick in your view"). It is also possible that an illness did afflict him at that time. And the interpretation of the verse is what we said about His saying: ﴿بَلْ فَعَلَهُ كَبِيرُهم هَذا فاسْألُوهم إنْ كانُوا يَنْطِقُونَ﴾.
Some argued that Ibrāhīm's saying is like Yūsuf's saying to his brothers: ﴿أيَّتُها العِيرُ إنَّكم لَسارِقُونَ﴾ [Yūsuf: 70], when they had not stolen the measuring cup; and its interpretation — and Allah knows best — is: ”إنَّكم لَسارِقُونَ يُوسُفَ“ ("you are the thieves of Yūsuf").
